From b89d88d391ba9caf58164a5d2cafb3ecbaa90105 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wings-cgroups <wings-cgroups@localhost>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 18:19:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/13] Distinguish a bare connection drop from a failed auth
 attempt in SFTP logging

Separate patch from 0010, deliberately: 0010 quiets a client that attempted
and failed authentication (*ssh.ServerAuthError). This handles a different,
equally common shape -- a client that connects and disconnects (cleanly or
via a network-level reset) BEFORE ever reaching the auth loop, e.g. during
the SSH version exchange, key exchange, or the initial service request. That
is the signature of a bare TCP port scanner (masscan, zmap, a misdirected
health check): connect, maybe read the banner, then go away without sending
anything back.

Verified against the exact pinned golang.org/x/crypto/ssh v0.46.0 source
that this case surfaces differently from 0010's: exchangeVersions/waitSession
read raw bytes off the connection and return whatever io error the net.Conn
produces -- io.EOF, io.ErrUnexpectedEOF, or a *net.OpError -- completely
unwrapped, never as *ssh.ServerAuthError, because nothing about credentials
was ever exchanged for the ssh library to have an opinion on. Before this
patch AcceptInbound still wrapped it with errors.WithStack like any other
failure and it logged at Error with a full stack trace, same as 0010's
symptom but for a different root cause.

isHandshakeDisconnect classifies this narrowly -- only io.EOF/
io.ErrUnexpectedEOF/*net.OpError, i.e. "the connection went away", never any
error the ssh library or Wings' own protocol logic actively raised -- so it
cannot absorb a genuine protocol bug (malformed packets, algorithm mismatch)
into routine scanner noise. logAcceptInboundFailure logs it at Warn under
"reason", same as 0010's case, but with a DISTINCT message
("sftp: connection dropped before authentication began" vs
"sftp: rejected unauthenticated connection") specifically so connection-level
scanning and credential-probing can be told apart and alerted on
independently -- operator's explicit ask: treat this with the same
seriousness as a failed-auth attempt, not as silence, and keep it separately
monitorable.

Filed as its own patch, not folded into 0010, so each can be independently
accepted or rejected if this series is ever proposed upstream -- they touch
the same function but are separable in intent and in the underlying error
taxonomy the ssh library exposes.

Test coverage: a table-driven unit test of isHandshakeDisconnect covers all
four accepted shapes plus three explicit non-matches (a plain unrelated
error, *ssh.ServerAuthError itself, nil), and two integration-shaped tests
through the real logAcceptInboundFailure call site (mirroring AcceptInbound's
actual errors.WithStack wrapping). Killed by mutation, twice: deleting the
whole branch fails both integration tests; narrowing the classifier to drop
the *net.OpError check fails the unit test and the OpError integration test.
Both restored after confirming the kill.

Also corrects 0010's own doc comment, which said a disconnecting client
"surfaces ... as a *ssh.ServerAuthError" unconditionally -- true only for a
disconnect partway through the auth loop (EOF while reading a userauth
packet, inside serverAuthenticate); a disconnect before the auth loop is
reached is exactly this patch's case. Sharpened, not rewritten.

